  "response": "Adjudicating between a drug reaction and an underlying autoimmune hepatitis begins with the chronology of the episode, and the question is a practical one, since the two diagnoses diverge in management: one demands lifelong avoidance of a compound, the other sustained immunosuppression. The latency between the first dose of the medication and the onset of jaundice was six weeks; in other words, jaundice appeared about a month and a half after the patient took the first dose of the medication.\n\nThe Roussel Uclaf Causality Assessment Method awards points for latency, the course of enzymes after drug withdrawal, concomitant drug use, exclusion of competing causes, and prior published reports of hepatotoxicity for the agent, and that tariff of points shows that the method is a structured scoring instrument used to grade the likelihood that a drug caused a liver injury. That graded approach to likelihood shows that a score of 3 to 5 indicates highly probable drug causality. The serological work-up, by contrast, directs attention to the rival diagnosis rather than to the agent. An elevated serum IgG concentration supports a diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis, and that cannot be squared with the fact that nitrofurantoin and minocycline are recognised causes of drug-induced autoimmune-like hepatitis; the immunoglobulin criterion and the status of those agents cannot both hold. Antinuclear antibodies are absent in all patients with drug-induced liver injury, a position that cannot be squared with the standing of those two antibiotics as causes of an autoimmune-like drug reaction; one of the two must give way.\n\nHistology supplies the next discriminator, and the pathologist is asked to read the pattern of injury on its own terms before any clinical label is attached to it. Liver biopsy in autoimmune hepatitis typically shows interface hepatitis with a plasma cell-rich portal infiltrate, and that typical appearance cannot be squared with the fact that prominent eosinophilic infiltration and zonal hepatocellular necrosis on biopsy favour a drug-induced aetiology; the two readings of the biopsy cannot both stand. Drug-leaning features of that kind carry real discriminating weight, whereas the subsequent therapeutic course affords the clinician far less purchase on the distinction.\n\nBiochemical improvement after stopping the suspect medication constitutes a positive dechallenge supporting drug causality. Steroid responsiveness is a further element of the picture: corticosteroid therapy can produce biochemical remission in both drug-induced liver injury with autoimmune features and idiopathic autoimmune hepatitis. That positive dechallenge indicates that the newly started medication caused the patient's liver injury. At least one of two things is therefore true of this patient, and perhaps both are: that the medication was responsible for the hepatitis, and that the patient had underlying idiopathic autoimmune hepatitis. Sound practice accordingly withdraws the agent, documents the biochemical trajectory in detail, records the histological pattern, and keeps both formulations in play through prolonged follow-up, since a relapse after immunosuppression is tapered will often settle the matter that the initial presentation could not.",
